<p>Monitoring tells you whether a relational service is keeping its promises and how much room remains before it cannot. Capacity planning turns that room into a decision: what work must still fit, how quickly the allowance is being consumed and when somebody must start an intervention.</p>
<p>Follow the shop from <a href="/review/relational-next/operations/">Running a relational service</a>. Customers place orders, staff run reports and PostgreSQL retains the history needed for recovery. A queue can harm customers while the volume is nearly empty. A failed archive can fill that volume while every order still succeeds. The dashboard needs to show both.</p>
<p>The figures use invented service and planning values. A separate native PostgreSQL 18.6 exercise checks the observation queries, privileges and allocation arithmetic. The concurrent-workload and recovery articles supply their own bounded evidence; none of these short local runs establishes sustainable production capacity.</p>

