<h2 id="build-the-dashboard-the-deployment-can-support">Build the dashboard the deployment can support</h2>
<p>Begin with the application operation the service must support. For this example, the shop requires 99.9% of eligible order attempts to succeed within 500 ms over 30 days. A failed or late attempt counts once as bad. The expected ordinary peak is 120 intended arrivals a second. These are illustrative requirements to accept with the application owner, including which invalid requests are excluded and whether admission rejection counts as failure. Here an otherwise eligible rejected order does count.</p>
<p>Keep the parent’s failure requirements beside that objective: restore order-taking within ten minutes after reporting saturation; five minutes and no lost acknowledged orders for writer-machine loss; a separate restoration allowance for committed mistakes or location loss. Ordinary speed and recoverability need different evidence.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="A collection inventory for the shop"><table><caption>Proposed collection intervals, to validate for overhead and visibility</caption><thead><tr><th>Source and owner</th><th>Collect and use</th><th>Boundary to record</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Application owner</td><td>Continuous operation counts and latency histograms; ten-second dashboard windows. Detect user harm and admission queues.</td><td>Operation class, intended arrival, terminal outcome, retries, collector delay and missing samples</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Database operator</td><td>Activity every ten seconds, database counter deltas every minute. Route waits and changed physical work.</td><td>Monitoring role, query cost, reset identity and sampling gaps; brief waits may be missed</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Host or container operator</td><td>CPU, memory pressure, storage throughput, latency and free space every minute. Compare with the actual resource limits.</td><td>Host versus container, throttling, filesystem or volume, usable allocation and exporter access</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Recovery and topology owners</td><td>Archive and replica progress each minute; backup results and last accepted restore. Detect lost coverage or catch-up margin.</td><td>Which history, member, failure domain, target and freshness definition the signal covers</td></tr>
 <tr><td>External check and on-call owner</td><td>A controlled application read/write check each minute, outside the production telemetry path. Detect unavailable service or a silent collector.</td><td>Probe identity, route, timeout, cleanup and excluded payment or fulfilment effects</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Inventory what the deployment actually exposes. A self-managed service can combine engine views with host observations. A hosted service may expose provider metrics and events while withholding filesystem access or configuration changes. Record those gaps, provider support ownership and quota-increase lead times. For example, RDS <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/rds-metrics.html">DatabaseConnections counts client network connections</a> and excludes several engine and provider session categories; it need not equal every row in an engine session view. Label the definition instead of adding overlapping counts.</p>
<p>Verify collection as the actual monitoring identity. In our disposable database, an ordinary observer could see that the fixture session existed, but its state was null and its query was hidden. Granting <code>pg_read_all_stats</code> exposed that state and query; reading the orders table still failed. Reading <code>pg_ls_waldir()</code> also remained forbidden. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/predefined-roles.html">Statistics visibility</a> can reveal sensitive statement text, and does not confer every file-observation permission. Agree the required scope through <a href="/review/relational-next/security/">the access owner</a>; a restricted panel must say unavailable, rather than display zero.</p>

<h3 id="apply-the-workload-measures-to-one-service-view">Apply the workload measures to one service view</h3>
<p>Use <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/#count-arrivals-completions-and-outstanding-work-together">the workload article’s accounting boundary</a>. <strong>Offered</strong> work arrives on its intended schedule; <strong>admitted</strong> work enters active service. <strong>Useful completions</strong> satisfy the application action. Failures and rejections are separate terminal outcomes. <strong>Outstanding</strong> work has not reached a terminal outcome; its queued portion is still waiting for service. Count one logical operation once even if it retries several database requests. A timed-out caller can leave server work running, so retain that residual work separately.</p>
<p>Put operation latency beside admission waiting, driver-call latency, completions and outstanding work. Then show engine waits and deployment limits. A rate is work per elapsed time; a gauge is a value at a sampling point. Do not average p95 values from several collectors: combine compatible histogram populations or retain separate class distributions and sample counts. Every panel should choose an action or the next evidence to inspect.</p>
<p>Figure 1 shows two changed signals. Advance to the second sample with <em>Admission slows</em>, then switch to <em>History is retained</em>. The application panels and recovery panels lead to different owners.</p>
<CapacityFigure scene="dashboard" number={1}/>
<p>The first case keeps an admission queue visible even while individual database exchanges remain short. The second gives an early warning that order-success graphs cannot provide. Add release, workload-mix, engine-setting and maintenance markers to the same time axis; compare equivalent peaks, rather than this morning’s quiet period with yesterday’s sale.</p>
<Sql code={observe} label="PostgreSQL observations, with explicit timestamps and database scope"/>
<p>Take successive cumulative observations in fresh transactions. Retain elapsed time, server and database identity, reset fields and counter decreases before deriving a rate. Statistics can arrive late; short intervals magnify that uncertainty. Our own database’s commit counter moved from 2 to 15 around setup, observation and fixture work: that difference is not thirteen placed orders. Temporary bytes increased by 1,107,392 during a deliberately small sort. This is cumulative temporary-file traffic, not current occupied temporary space or a disk-speed measurement.</p>
<p>Activity is a sample, not a duration. An active backend may be waiting, and an empty wait field does not measure CPU use. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html">statistics definitions</a> and the deployment’s host metrics establish those boundaries. Increasing collection frequency or enabling additional timing needs an overhead check; a more expensive observer can change the service it observes.</p>

<h2 id="route-a-changed-signal-with-its-evidence">Route a changed signal with its evidence</h2>
<p>Keep the affected interval, including enough baseline to see what changed. Preserve counts and class distributions, missing-sample markers, release and job records, engine observations and relevant logs before cancellation, restart or reconfiguration removes them. The table selects the next procedure; a temporal coincidence alone does not establish a cause.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Changed signals and their owning procedures"><table><thead><tr><th>Changed signal</th><th>Retain</th><th>Route to</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Latency, queue or rejected work rises</td><td>Intended arrivals, useful/failure/rejection counts, outstanding work, per-class tails, activity and host window</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Concurrent-workload diagnosis</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>One statement class becomes expensive</td><td>Statement identity, representative parameters, plan, actual rows and physical-work evidence</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Query-plan diagnosis</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Version estimates or allocated files grow</td><td>Object sizes, transaction/snapshot horizons, upkeep progress and warnings</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/#diagnose-upkeep-before-choosing-a-command">Maintenance diagnosis</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>WAL grows or replica progress stalls</td><td>Allocated WAL, generated-byte trend, archive errors, slot status, member positions and time to reserve</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/#account-for-retained-logs">Retention owner</a>, then <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/#keep-the-stream-able-to-catch-up">replica</a> or <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/#keep-recovery-possible-after-production-fails">archive/recovery owner</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Writer unavailable or wrong member reached</td><td>External probe, connection destination, member state, acknowledgements and provider events</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/#replace-a-writer-without-leaving-two-authorities">Writer replacement</a></td></tr>
 <tr><td>Suspected damage or wrong committed facts</td><td>Errors, affected identifiers, change history, recovery coverage and protected comparison copies</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/operations/#recover-the-incident-without-destroying-the-evidence">Incident coordination</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">isolated restoration</a></td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="forecast-space-and-the-time-left-to-act">Forecast space and the time left to act</h2>
<p>Start with allocated bytes and free space on each actual storage boundary. Database object size explains some consumption; the volume or provider metric determines whether another write fits. Keep measurements dated, with units and collection permissions. The planning tables below use decimal GB and MB/s; the native evidence retains exact bytes.</p>
<Sql code={sizes} label="Object allocation and upkeep observations for the affected table"/>
<p><code>pg_total_relation_size</code> already includes the table and its indexes; adding all three columns would double-count. In the native fixture, 4,000 orders occupied 1,359,872 bytes in total. Deleting 3,000 and vacuuming with end truncation disabled left the main heap at 1,220,608 bytes. Total allocation rose by 8,192 bytes as the visibility map appeared. Fewer live rows did not mean more free volume space. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-admin.html#FUNCTIONS-ADMIN-DBSIZE">size-function definitions</a> tell you which allocations overlap; <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">maintenance</a> owns any justified reclamation procedure.</p>

<h3 id="include-retained-and-temporary-allocations">Include retained and temporary allocations</h3>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Capacity worksheet showing different allocation boundaries"><table><caption>Invented starting budget for one service; replace each value with a dated observation</caption><thead><tr><th>Allocation and boundary</th><th>Starting amount and assumption</th><th>Measurement and limit owner</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Local table/TOAST files and indexes</td><td>224 GB, including reusable interior space and retained row versions; ordinary net growth 4 GB/h</td><td>Object functions and volume reconciliation; database operator</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Local WAL</td><td>64 GB already allocated; adverse additional retention 8 GB/h until the consumer recovers</td><td>Permitted WAL-directory/provider allocation plus archive and slot progress; recovery/topology owners</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Other local allocation</td><td>16 GB for catalogue, service logs and other identified files</td><td>Host/provider volume inventory; platform operator</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Local free allocation</td><td>96 GB of a 400 GB usable boundary; keep 32 GB for the rehearsed temporary/change peak</td><td>Volume/provider free space; expansion or quota owner</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Remote recovery copies</td><td>900 GB retained independently; seven-day recovery coverage plus required older base and investigation time</td><td>Backup catalogue, actual retained bytes and expiry chain; recovery owner and storage-account quota owner</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Separate restore destination</td><td>Proposed 500 GB usable staging; exact peak and provisioning time still unverified</td><td>Restore rehearsal and destination quota; recovery/platform owners</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The local rows reconcile: 224 + 64 + 16 + 96 = 400 GB. Remote copies and a separate destination do not consume that local 96 GB. Shared or incremental snapshot billing can overlap physical data, so use the provider’s actual retained-allocation and quota definitions rather than multiplying logical database size by copy count.</p>
<p>Do not add dead-version estimates as a second allocation: in PostgreSQL those versions already occupy table files. Likewise, WAL needed by several slots is overlapping history; adding each position distance overstates allocated WAL. A slot’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/view-pg-replication-slots.html"><code>restart_lsn</code> and <code>wal_status</code></a> help explain retention, while the allocation measure answers the space question. Keep archive failure, lagging slots and relevant snapshot horizons with their owners.</p>
<p>Budget transient peaks before a job begins: sorts, ingestion, a replacement heap and indexes, upgrade copies, restore staging and log generated during the work. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/#fit-maintenance-within-the-service-budget">the maintenance budget</a> and <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/#rehearse-a-full-restore-in-isolation">the measured restore path</a> for the selected procedure. A reserved 32 GB is still free today, but is unavailable for ordinary growth if tomorrow’s required intervention needs it.</p>

<h3 id="compare-growth-with-intervention-lead-time">Compare growth with intervention lead time</h3>
<p>For positive net growth, <code>time to reserve = (free − required reserve) ÷ growth rate</code>. Also calculate <code>time to hard full = free ÷ growth rate</code>. If growth is zero or negative, there is no finite linear exhaustion forecast; bursts and required work still need an allowance. Derive ordinary growth from comparable observed intervals, then adjust explicitly for changed ingestion, retention or indexes. Use a credible adverse rate, rather than treating an average as a bound.</p>
<p>The example’s intervention takes eight hours: up to one hour to detect, one to investigate, four to provision and two to execute and validate. Add two hours for uncertainty. The action threshold is therefore <code>32 + 4 × 10 = 72 GB free</code> ordinarily, and <code>32 + 12 × 10 = 152 GB</code> with blocked archival. Figure 2 advances both forecasts. Then extend the intervention to sixteen hours.</p>
<CapacityFigure scene="space" number={2}/>
<p>At the invented observation time, 20 October at 10:00, ordinary growth leaves sixteen hours to the reserve. Start action by 16:00 and review the forecast by 14:00. Blocked archival leaves only 5.3 hours to the reserve, already less than the ten-hour allowance: act now and escalate the missing margin. The hard limit is eight hours away, but those final 32 GB are needed to complete the intervention.</p>
<p>Record what expansion can actually do. RDS storage autoscaling has a configured maximum and <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIOPS.Autoscaling.html">restrictions on when further modifications can occur</a>. It does not eliminate quota, large-ingestion or retention risk. Include the exact deployment’s restrictions and provider lead time in the worksheet. A larger permitted maximum is not immediately usable free space. Never improvise removal of engine log files to make the forecast fit.</p>

<h2 id="reserve-capacity-for-normal-and-degraded-operation">Reserve capacity for normal and degraded operation</h2>
<p>Space is only one envelope. Record CPU, memory, storage latency and throughput, connections and permitted active work at ordinary peaks. Keep configured ceilings distinct from useful observed capacity. A connection maximum is not a target for active queries, and a provisioned I/O number is not a guarantee that every query receives that rate.</p>
<p>Now add the scenarios the service must survive: a promoted writer inherits traffic; a replica catches up; backup or restore reads compete with foreground work; maintenance overlaps an ordinary peak. Decide which combinations must retain ordinary objectives and which permit reduced service. The example permits reports to be deferred for at most 30 minutes during member loss while order entry retains its objective. That policy needs an owner and a way to bound the deferred backlog.</p>
<p>Figure 3 adds inherited reads and catch-up to one proposed resource budget. At the final step, switch to <em>Defer agreed reporting</em> and compare every resource.</p>
<CapacityFigure scene="envelope" number={3}/>
<p>Keeping all work exceeds three proposed limits. Deferring reports makes these sums fit, but that arithmetic does not prove latency, memory safety or catch-up time. The reservations are invented allowances. Real CPU and I/O costs can change with concurrency, cache state and data size. Memory needs process and operation overlap as well as caches; connections need operational access left available across all application instances. Record the workload evidence supporting each allowance and the provisioning delay if the next tested size is required.</p>

<h3 id="accept-the-budget-against-existing-evidence">Accept the budget against existing evidence</h3>
<p><a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/#test-the-proposed-limit-without-hiding-the-overload">The existing workload comparison</a> offered 240 operations on a two-second schedule at 120/s. Before its transaction-boundary repair, 187–188 completed by two seconds and 52–53 remained. Afterwards, all 240 completed within the schedule; no error or rejection occurred. That supports the fixture’s repair, not the capacity figures above. It used tiny data on a shared PostgreSQL 18.6 instance and collected no host CPU, total-memory or device-I/O envelope.</p>
<p>The neighbours’ evidence is bounded too. <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/#rehearse-the-failures-that-determine-the-choice">The local switchover</a> reached a new accepted write in 0.303 seconds, excluding failure detection, remote infrastructure and the real application pool. <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/#rehearse-a-full-restore-in-isolation">The tiny restore</a> reached queries in under a second, excluding most provisioning, retrieval and service validation. Neither proves the shop’s five-minute or sixty-minute complete path, or its proposed 500 GB restore destination.</p>
<p>Accept a production budget only when evidence matches its data size, skew, arrival pattern, deployment, background work and duration. For this proposed service, require the stated per-class success and latency objective at ordinary peaks, bounded outstanding work, no exhausted resource allowance and the selected failure’s loss and complete recovery-time bound. For reduced reporting, additionally prove the agreed deferral and subsequent drain time. Missing sustained workload, maintenance-overlap or actual-provider recovery evidence is a targeted recheck for the owning procedure. The honest status of this worksheet is <strong>planned, not accepted</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="turn-capacity-and-service-alerts-into-owned-action">Turn capacity and service alerts into owned action</h2>
<p>Page an owner when sustained user harm is spending the allowed failure budget rapidly, or required capacity will run out before an intervention can finish. Schedule slower growth and upkeep work with a deadline. A universal CPU or free-space percentage cannot select that urgency on its own.</p>
<p>For the illustrative order objective, the allowed bad fraction is 0.1%. A current 1% bad fraction is ten times that allowance. Retain request counts and overlapping short and long windows so a brief blip, a persistent small regression and an outage remain distinguishable. The ratio describes the current rate of spending relative to the allowance; variable demand means it is not a direct measure of the fraction of a monthly request budget already spent.</p>
<p>Figure 4 follows the handoff from detection through verification. Switch to the missing-collector case: an absent metric must not turn the service green.</p>
<CapacityFigure scene="alerts" number={4}/>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Example alert ownership and deadlines"><table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Owner, evidence and deadline</th><th>Procedure and completion</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Bad order fraction breaches agreed sustained windows</td><td>Service on-call; class counts, tails, queue and changes. Deliver immediately, acknowledge within five minutes; escalate if unacknowledged.</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">Workload response</a> within the ten-minute order-taking budget; verify useful work and latency</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Time to required space reserve ≤ intervention plus contingency</td><td>Database/platform on-call; allocations, growth and retained-history consumer. Start now; escalate immediately if the supported path cannot fit.</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/#account-for-retained-logs">Retention</a> or supported expansion; remeasure margin without sacrificing recovery coverage</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Ordinary forecast reaches its dated action threshold</td><td>Capacity owner; worksheet and provider quota. In the worked case review by 14:00, start by 16:00.</td><td>Scheduled capacity work; verify the usable allocation and revised forecast</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Collection has missed two expected one-minute samples</td><td>Telemetry owner and service on-call; last timestamp, exporter status and external check. Investigate immediately; escalate on service impact or continuing unknown state.</td><td>Repair collection and compare independent outcomes; advancing samples required</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Restore evidence or future partition coverage is about to expire</td><td>Recovery/topology owner; latest accepted rehearsal, catalogue and next required boundary. Deadline precedes expiry by the documented preparation allowance.</td><td><a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">Restore</a> or <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/#choose-between-local-partitions-and-distributed-ownership">partition lifecycle</a>; prove renewed coverage</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Verify the alert path before relying on it. In a bounded test, inject a labelled synthetic breach into a test route, confirm delivery to the intended person, acknowledgement, escalation and recovery notification, then remove it. Separately stop only the test collector to check the missing-data route. A successful rule evaluation is not proof of delivery. These are acceptance tasks for the shop’s actual monitoring system; this article’s native exercise did not contact an alerting service or notify anyone.</p>
<p>After an intervention, check application outcomes and the affected capacity clock. If workload diagnosis is needed, let <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/#match-the-intervention-to-the-limiting-resource">its owner compare reducing work, changing limits and adding resources</a>; let the topology owner decide placement changes. Update this worksheet after material changes to demand, retained history, engine, deployment or recovery path. Record who accepts the new assumption and when its evidence must be renewed.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Checked on 20 October 2026. All figures and large capacity values are labelled models or unverified planning assumptions. The observation exercise ran on native PostgreSQL 18.6 on one shared Apple-silicon laptop; it created and removed only its own database and monitoring role, without resetting statistics or changing server configuration.</p>
<p><a href={budgetUrl} download="capacity-worksheet.csv">Download the capacity worksheet</a> and replace invented values with your dated observations. To repeat the native checks, <a href={worksheetUrl} download="monitoring-verify.mjs">download the observation worksheet</a> and <a href={evidenceUrl} download="monitoring-evidence.json">the exact retained evidence</a>. Use Node.js 22 or later, install <code>pg@8.23.1</code> with lifecycle scripts disabled in a fresh directory, set <code>PG_URL</code> to an administrative connection on a disposable PostgreSQL 18 server, then run <code>node monitoring-verify.mjs</code>. It needs database- and role-creation authority and up to three connections; it refuses to overwrite its evidence file and removes its own database and role after closing the sessions.</p>
<ul>
 <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring-stats.html">Cumulative statistics</a> defines the observations; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/monitoring.html">monitoring database activity</a> places engine and host tools together.</li>
 <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/predefined-roles.html">Predefined roles</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-admin.html">administration functions</a> define monitoring privileges, size functions and restricted directory observations.</li>
 <li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/view-pg-replication-slots.html">Replication slots</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html">continuous archiving</a> define retained-history obligations. Operating procedures remain with maintenance, topology and restoration.</li>
 <li>Amazon RDS: <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/rds-metrics.html">CloudWatch metric definitions</a> and <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIOPS.Autoscaling.html">storage autoscaling</a> supply the specific hosted examples; no RDS metrics, expansion or failure was tested.</li>
</ul>
